WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.220
A judge from Morocco.

2
00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:04.790
In a city he had never seen.

3
00:00:05.258 --> 00:00:07.658
Ibn Battuta was born in Tangier,

4
00:00:07.658 --> 00:00:10.318
into a family of legal scholars.

5
00:00:10.318 --> 00:00:12.718
By thirteen thirty three, after

6
00:00:12.718 --> 00:00:14.658
years of travel, he reached

7
00:00:14.658 --> 00:00:17.018
Sind. The Sultan of Delhi,

8
00:00:17.018 --> 00:00:18.778
Muhammad bin Tughlaq, was

9
00:00:18.778 --> 00:00:20.278
impressed by his scholarship,

10
00:00:20.278 --> 00:00:22.758
and made him qazi, the judge of

11
00:00:22.758 --> 00:00:23.558
Delhi.

12
00:00:23.955 --> 00:00:26.395
He found Delhi vast, the largest

13
00:00:26.395 --> 00:00:27.575
city in India.

14
00:00:27.575 --> 00:00:29.995
Its rampart was eleven cubits

15
00:00:29.995 --> 00:00:32.055
thick, with twenty eight gates,

16
00:00:32.055 --> 00:00:34.295
and storehouses for grain inside

17
00:00:34.295 --> 00:00:35.345
the wall.

18
00:00:35.811 --> 00:00:37.391
He admired the post.

19
00:00:37.391 --> 00:00:40.111
A horse post, the uluq, with

20
00:00:40.111 --> 00:00:42.351
royal horses every four miles.

21
00:00:42.351 --> 00:00:45.271
And a foot post, the dawa, where

22
00:00:45.271 --> 00:00:46.951
runners shook a rod of copper

23
00:00:46.951 --> 00:00:49.151
bells, and the next runner took

24
00:00:49.151 --> 00:00:50.211
the letter.

25
00:00:50.211 --> 00:00:51.861
Faster than horses.

26
00:00:52.389 --> 00:00:54.369
How do you describe a paan to

27
00:00:54.369 --> 00:00:56.309
readers who have never seen one?

28
00:00:56.309 --> 00:00:58.929
He compared the betel plant to a

29
00:00:58.929 --> 00:00:59.689
grape vine.

30
00:00:59.689 --> 00:01:01.989
Travel writing, fourteenth

31
00:01:01.989 --> 00:01:03.299
century style.

32
00:01:03.924 --> 00:01:06.224
Then he fell out of favour, and

33
00:01:06.224 --> 00:01:07.984
was thrown into prison.

34
00:01:07.984 --> 00:01:10.024
When the misunderstanding was

35
00:01:10.024 --> 00:01:12.064
cleared, he was restored to

36
00:01:12.064 --> 00:01:14.177
service, and in thirteen forty

37
00:01:14.177 --> 00:01:16.824
two, sent as the Sultan's envoy

38
00:01:16.824 --> 00:01:17.854
to China.

39
00:01:18.427 --> 00:01:20.767
He came home in thirteen fifty

40
00:01:20.767 --> 00:01:22.827
four, about thirty years after

41
00:01:22.827 --> 00:01:23.767
setting out.

42
00:01:23.767 --> 00:01:26.387
Then, at a ruler's request, he

43
00:01:26.387 --> 00:01:29.270
dictated his account, the Rihla.

44
00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:30.830
For GS1:

45
00:01:30.830 --> 00:01:33.890
Ibn Battuta, a Moroccan, qazi of

46
00:01:33.890 --> 00:01:35.470
Delhi under Muhammad bin

47
00:01:35.470 --> 00:01:37.430
Tughlaq. His Rihla describes

48
00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:40.230
cities, the uluq and dawa post,

49
00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:41.070
and goods.

50
00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:43.510
It is a traveller's source, so

51
00:01:43.510 --> 00:01:44.840
read it with care.

52
00:01:45.588 --> 00:01:47.388
A judge by day.

53
00:01:47.388 --> 00:01:49.658
A witness for the centuries.
